-- title:
--   §2, (2.2)–(2.6), Definition 2.2, (4.11), pp. 5–7, 14 — Brownian motion, regime chain, Itô integral, controls, X ∘ dI, admissible set, payoff J, stationary MFE, barrier control
-- statement:
--   The stochastic model of Aïd, Basei and Ferrari.
--
--   **Setting (p. 5).** On a complete probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$ live a real Brownian motion $B$ and a two-state Markov chain $\varepsilon$ with generator $P$ of (2.1), independent of $B$; both initial regimes have positive probability. $\mathbb F$ is the filtration generated by $(B,\varepsilon)$, augmented by the $\mathbb P$-null sets, and $M_t=\int_0^t\sigma_{\varepsilon_s}\,dB_s$ is the Itô integral, taken with continuous paths and $M_0=0$. The expectation $\mathbb E_{(x,i)}$ is the expectation under $\mathbb P(\,\cdot\mid\varepsilon_0=i)$; the initial capacity $x>0$ enters through the formulas below.
--
--   **Controls and state (pp. 6).** A control $I\in\mathcal M$ is $\mathbb F$-adapted, nonnegative, with nondecreasing càdlàg paths and $I_{0-}=0$ (so a jump at time $0$ is allowed). The controlled capacity is (2.4):
--   $$X^I_t=x\exp\Big(-\Big(\delta t+\frac12\int_0^t\sigma^2_{\varepsilon_s}ds\Big)+\int_0^t\sigma_{\varepsilon_s}dB_s+I_t\Big).$$
--   The investment integral (2.3) is
--   $$\int_{[0,\cdot]}X^I_t\circ dI_t=\int_0^\cdot X^I_t\,dI^c_t+\sum_{t\le\cdot}X^I_{t-}\big(e^{\Delta I_t}-1\big).$$
--
--   **Admissibility and payoff (2.5)–(2.6).** $I\in\mathcal A$ if $I\in\mathcal M$,
--   $$\lim_{T\to\infty}\mathbb E_{(x,i)}\big[e^{-\rho T}|X^I_T|^2\big]=0\quad\text{and}\quad \mathbb E_{(x,i)}\Big[\int_0^\infty e^{-\rho t}\big((X^I_t)^2dt+X^I_t\circ dI_t\big)\Big]<\infty,$$
--   and for $Q\in(0,\infty)^2$ the net profit is
--   $$J_{(x,i)}(I,Q)=\mathbb E_{(x,i)}\Big[\int_0^\infty e^{-\rho t}\big(\eta_{\varepsilon_t}X^I_t\,dt-c(X^I_t)^2dt-\kappa X^I_t\circ dI_t\big)\Big].$$
--
--   **Stationary mean-field equilibrium (Definition 2.2).** $(I^\star,Q^\star)$ with $Q^\star\in(0,\infty)^2$ is an MFE for the data $(x,i)$ if
--   1. $I^\star\in\mathcal A$ and $J_{(x,i)}(I^\star,Q^\star)\ge J_{(x,i)}(I,Q^\star)$ for every $I\in\mathcal A$;
--   2. $(X^{I^\star}_t,\varepsilon_t)_{t\ge0}$ admits a stationary distribution $p_\infty$ and, for $j=1,2$,
--   $$Q^\star_j=\frac1{\pi_j}\int_0^\infty x\,p_\infty(dx,j).$$
--
--   **Barrier control (4.11).** For barriers $a_1,a_2>0$,
--   $$I^\star_t=0\vee\sup_{0\le s\le t}\Big(\ln\big(a_{\varepsilon_s}/x\big)+\int_0^s\big(\delta+\tfrac12\sigma^2_{\varepsilon_u}\big)du-\int_0^s\sigma_{\varepsilon_u}dB_u\Big),\qquad I^\star_{0-}=0.$$
--
--   These objects state the paper's equilibrium problem from the model itself: optimality is against every admissible control and the stationary distribution is that of the actually controlled process.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Regimes are `Fin 2` (index `0` = regime 1). Time is $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$. $M$ is characterized by the platform relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` with integrand $\sigma_{\varepsilon}$. The integral (2.3) is computed as $\int_{[0,\infty)}e^{-\rho t}Y_t\,d(e^{I})_t$, where $Y=Xe^{-I}$ is the (continuous) uncontrolled factor and $d(e^{I})$ is the Stieltjes measure of the path $t\mapsto e^{I_t}$ extended by $1$ before time $0$; this equals (2.3) term by term, including the initial jump $x(e^{I_0}-1)$. Both expectations in $\mathcal A$ are lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$, so they cannot take junk values; $J$ is a real (Bochner) expectation and is only compared on admissible controls, for which it is integrable. "Admits a stationary distribution $p_\infty$" (never defined on the page) is read as: $p_\infty$ is a probability measure on $\mathbb R\times\{1,2\}$ and the law of $(X^{I}_t,\varepsilon_t)$ under $\mathbb E_{(x,i)}$ converges weakly to it as $t\to\infty$. $Q_j>0$ is required because $\eta_j$ is infinite at $Q_j=0$. The barrier control is used only with $a_j>0$, where $\ln(a_j/x)$ is defined.
-- source:
--   Aïd, Basei & Ferrari, A Stationary Mean-Field Equilibrium Model of Irreversible Investment in a Two-Regime Economy, arXiv:2305.00541v1, pp. 5–7, (2.1)–(2.6), Definition 2.2; p. 14, (4.11)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_HasBrownianItoIntegral
import Definitions.Def_MFIrrev_Equilibrium_TwoStateChain
import Definitions.Def_MFIrrev_Equilibrium_Analytic

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Set
open scoped NNReal ENNReal Topology

namespace MFIrrev.Equilibrium

/-- The random objects of §2 on a measurable space `Ω`: the Brownian motion `B`, the regime chain
`ε` (index `0` = regime 1), the process `M_t = ∫₀ᵗ σ_{ε_s} dB_s` and the filtration `𝔽`. -/
structure Setting (Ω : Type) [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] where
  /-- the Brownian motion `B` -/
  B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  /-- the regime chain `ε` -/
  ε : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin 2
  /-- the stochastic integral `M_t = ∫₀ᵗ σ_{ε_s} dB_s` -/
  M : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  /-- the filtration `𝔽` -/
  ℱ : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ

/-- The standing probabilistic assumptions of p. 5 (and the Itô integral of (2.4)):
1. `P` is a complete probability measure;
2. `B` is a real Brownian motion;
3. `ε` is a two-state Markov chain with generator (2.1) with respect to `𝔽`;
4. both initial regimes have positive probability, so that `E_(x,i)` is defined;
5. `B` and `ε` are independent (as path-valued random variables);
6. `𝔽` is the filtration generated by `(B, ε)`, augmented by the `P`-null sets;
7. `M` is the Itô integral `∫₀^· σ_{ε_s} dB_s` (platform relation `HasBrownianItoIntegral`), taken in
   its version with continuous paths and `M₀ = 0`. -/
def IsSetting (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure P ∧ P.IsComplete ∧
  IsBrownianReal S.B P ∧
  IsTwoStateChain P S.ℱ m.p S.ε ∧
  (∀ i : Fin 2, 0 < P (S.ε 0 ⁻¹' {i})) ∧
  IndepFun (fun ω t => S.B t ω) (fun ω t => S.ε t ω) P ∧
  (∀ t : ℝ≥0, S.ℱ t =
    (⨆ s ≤ t, (MeasurableSpace.comap (S.B s) inferInstance
                ⊔ MeasurableSpace.comap (S.ε s) inferInstance))
      ⊔ MeasurableSpace.generateFrom {A | MeasurableSet A ∧ P A = 0}) ∧
  EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral P (fun t => S.ℱ t) S.B (fun t ω => m.σ (S.ε t ω)) S.M ∧
  (∀ ω, Continuous fun t => S.M t ω) ∧ (∀ ω, S.M 0 ω = 0)

/-- `P_(x,i)`: the measure `P` conditioned on `ε₀ = i` (the initial capacity `x` enters through the
formulas). `E_(x,i)` is the integral against it. -/
noncomputable def Pcond {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω) (i : Fin 2) :
    Measure Ω :=
  ProbabilityTheory.cond P (S.ε 0 ⁻¹' {i})

/-- `∫₀ᵗ σ²_{ε_s} ds`. -/
noncomputable def sigSqInt (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω) (t : ℝ≥0)
    (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  ∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..(t : ℝ), m.σ (S.ε s.toNNReal ω) ^ 2

/-- The uncontrolled capacity `Y_t = x exp(−(δt + ½∫₀ᵗσ²_{ε_s}ds) + ∫₀ᵗσ_{ε_s}dB_s)`. -/
noncomputable def Y (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω) (x : ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0)
    (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  x * Real.exp (-(m.δ * t + sigSqInt m S t ω / 2) + S.M t ω)

/-- The controlled capacity (2.4): `X^I_t = x exp(−(δt + ½∫₀ᵗσ²_{ε_s}ds) + ∫₀ᵗσ_{ε_s}dB_s + I_t)`. -/
noncomputable def X (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω) (x : ℝ)
    (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  x * Real.exp (-(m.δ * t + sigSqInt m S t ω / 2) + S.M t ω + I t ω)

/-- The class `𝓜` (p. 6): `I` is `𝔽`-adapted, nonnegative, with nondecreasing right-continuous
(hence càdlàg) paths. `I_{0−} = 0` is the convention that the path is `0` before time `0`, so
`I₀ > 0` is an initial jump. -/
def IsControl {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω) (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ t, Measurable[S.ℱ t] (I t)) ∧ (∀ t ω, 0 ≤ I t ω) ∧ (∀ ω, Monotone fun t => I t ω) ∧
  (∀ ω t, ContinuousWithinAt (fun s => I s ω) (Ici t) t)

/-- The path of `I` extended to `ℝ` by `I_{0−} = 0` (value `0` at negative times). -/
noncomputable def ext {Ω : Type} (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (ω : Ω) : ℝ → ℝ :=
  fun t => if t < 0 then 0 else I t.toNNReal ω

open Classical in
/-- The Stieltjes measure of the nondecreasing path `t ↦ e^{I_t}` (extended by `e^{I_{0−}} = 1`
before time `0`), so that it carries the mass `e^{I₀} − 1` at `0`; the zero measure if the path is
not monotone (never the case for a control). -/
noncomputable def expMeasure {Ω : Type} (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (ω : Ω) : Measure ℝ :=
  if h : Monotone (fun t => Real.exp (ext I ω t)) then h.stieltjesFunction.measure else 0

/-- `∫_{[0,∞)} e^{−rt} X^I_t ∘ dI_t` of (2.3), with values in `ℝ≥0∞`. It is computed as
`∫_{[0,∞)} e^{−rt} Y_t d(e^{I})_t`, which equals (2.3): `X = Y e^{I}` with `Y` continuous gives
`X_t dI^c_t = Y_t d(e^{I})^c_t` on the continuous part and
`X_{t−}(e^{ΔI_t} − 1) = Y_t (e^{I_t} − e^{I_{t−}})` at each jump, including the initial jump
`x(e^{I₀} − 1)` (`Y₀ = x = X_{0−}`). -/
noncomputable def circInt (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω) (x : ℝ)
    (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (r : ℝ) (ω : Ω) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ∫⁻ t in Ici (0 : ℝ), ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-r * t) * Y m S x t.toNNReal ω) ∂(expMeasure I ω)

/-- The admissible set `𝒜` of (2.5), for the data `(x, i)`: `I ∈ 𝓜`,
`lim_{T→∞} E_(x,i)[e^{−ρT}|X^I_T|²] = 0` and
`E_(x,i)[∫₀^∞ e^{−ρt}((X^I_t)² dt + X^I_t ∘ dI_t)] < ∞`; both expectations are `ℝ≥0∞`-valued
(lower Lebesgue integrals of nonnegative quantities). -/
def IsAdmissible (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω)
    (x : ℝ) (i : Fin 2) (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsControl S I ∧
  Tendsto (fun T : ℝ≥0 =>
      ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-m.ρ * T) * X m S x I T ω ^ 2) ∂(Pcond P S i))
    atTop (𝓝 0) ∧
  ∫⁻ ω, ((∫⁻ t in Ici (0 : ℝ), ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-m.ρ * t) * X m S x I t.toNNReal ω ^ 2))
          + circInt m S x I m.ρ ω) ∂(Pcond P S i) < ⊤

/-- The net profit functional (2.6):
`J_(x,i)(I, Q) = E_(x,i)[∫₀^∞ e^{−ρt}(η_{ε_t} X^I_t dt − c (X^I_t)² dt − κ X^I_t ∘ dI_t)]`, with
`η` the inverse demand (2.7) at `Q`. It is used only on admissible controls, for which every term
is integrable. -/
noncomputable def J (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω)
    (x : ℝ) (i : Fin 2) (Q : Fin 2 → ℝ) (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ ω, ((∫ t in Ici (0 : ℝ), Real.exp (-m.ρ * t) *
          (eta m Q (S.ε t.toNNReal ω) * X m S x I t.toNNReal ω
            - m.c * X m S x I t.toNNReal ω ^ 2))
        - m.κ * (circInt m S x I m.ρ ω).toReal) ∂(Pcond P S i)

/-- "`(X^I_t, ε_t)_{t≥0}` admits the stationary distribution `μ`", read as: `μ` is a probability
measure on `ℝ × {regimes}` and the law of `(X^I_t, ε_t)` under `P_(x,i)` converges weakly to `μ` as
`t → ∞` (tested against bounded continuous functions). -/
def HasLimitLaw (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω)
    (x : ℝ) (i : Fin 2) (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (μ : Measure (ℝ × Fin 2)) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧
  ∀ f : BoundedContinuousFunction (ℝ × Fin 2) ℝ,
    Tendsto (fun t : ℝ≥0 => ∫ ω, f (X m S x I t ω, S.ε t ω) ∂(Pcond P S i)) atTop
      (𝓝 (∫ z, f z ∂μ))

/-- Definition 2.2 (stationary mean-field equilibrium for the data `(x, i)`), with `Q ∈ (0, ∞)²`:
1. `I` is admissible and maximizes `J_(x,i)(·, Q)` over all admissible controls;
2. `(X^I_t, ε_t)` admits a stationary distribution `μ` (limit law) under which `x 𝟙{regime j}` is
   integrable and `Q_j = π_j⁻¹ ∫ x μ(dx, j)` for both regimes `j`. -/
def IsMFE (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (S : Setting Ω) (x : ℝ)
    (i : Fin 2) (I : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (Q : Fin 2 → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ j, 0 < Q j) ∧ IsAdmissible m P S x i I ∧
  (∀ I', IsAdmissible m P S x i I' → J m P S x i Q I' ≤ J m P S x i Q I) ∧
  ∃ μ : Measure (ℝ × Fin 2), HasLimitLaw m P S x i I μ ∧ ∀ j : Fin 2,
    Integrable (fun z : ℝ × Fin 2 => if z.2 = j then z.1 else 0) μ ∧
    Q j = (piStat m j)⁻¹ * ∫ z, (if z.2 = j then z.1 else 0) ∂μ

/-- The barrier control (4.11) at barriers `a` (used only with `0 < a j`):
`I⋆_t = 0 ∨ sup_{0≤s≤t}(ln(a_{ε_s}/x) + ∫₀ˢ(δ + ½σ²_{ε_u})du − ∫₀ˢσ_{ε_u}dB_u)`, where
`∫₀ˢ(δ + ½σ²_{ε_u})du = δs + ½∫₀ˢσ²_{ε_u}du`. The supremum is over the compact interval `[0, t]` of
a bounded function (`M` has continuous paths and `a` takes two values), so it is a genuine
supremum. -/
noncomputable def barrier (m : Params) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setting Ω)
    (a : Fin 2 → ℝ) (x : ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  max 0 (⨆ s : Icc (0 : ℝ≥0) t,
    (Real.log (a (S.ε s ω) / x) + (m.δ * ((s : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) + sigSqInt m S s ω / 2) - S.M s ω))

end MFIrrev.Equilibrium


